# PLAN: Fully Browser-Based Serverless AI (No API, No Backend)

## Concept
The AI model runs inside the visitor's browser on their own GPU or CPU.
No server, no API key, no per-request cost. The "server" is a static website.

    Static website (HTML/JS) -> downloads model weights once -> browser cache
            |
    WebGPU (GPU) or WASM (CPU fallback) runs the model locally
            |
    Chat UI + history saved in IndexedDB

Hosting: GitHub Pages / Cloudflare Pages / Netlify (free).
Privacy bonus: user prompts never leave the device.

Honest limit: browser models are small (about 0.5B to 4B parameters).
They will not match full-size Claude, DeepSeek, Qwen or Kimi, but they are
good for focused tasks.


## PHASE 0: Goal and limits (Week 1)
[ ] Pick the use case: general chat / study helper / code explainer / document Q&A
[ ] Write 20-30 test prompts to re-run after every change
[ ] Set targets: first-load size, tokens per second, minimum supported device


## PHASE 1: Choose the runtime (Week 1)
[ ] WebLLM (MLC)      - fastest chat on WebGPU, OpenAI-style JS interface, streaming
[ ] Transformers.js   - many task types (chat, classify, embeddings, speech)
[ ] wllama            - llama.cpp in WASM, CPU-only fallback for any device
[ ] Decision: WebLLM as primary, wllama as fallback


## PHASE 2: Choose the model (Week 1-2)
[ ] Start with a 4-bit quantized model around 1B to 3B (Qwen small instruct,
    Llama small, Phi, Gemma small). Check each license.
[ ] Size guide: ~1B = about 0.5-1 GB download, ~3B = about 2 GB
[ ] Compare 2-3 candidates on the test prompts, then choose
[ ] Add a model picker (Small / Medium) so weak devices still work
[ ] Use the model IDs from the library's current model list (they change often)


## PHASE 3: Build the website (Week 2-3)
[ ] Loading screen with download progress bar
[ ] Chat UI: streaming tokens, stop button, copy button, markdown + code blocks
[ ] Run the model in a Web Worker so the page never freezes
[ ] Device check: detect WebGPU and memory, auto-pick model or fallback
[ ] Chat history in IndexedDB
[ ] Dark mode, mobile-friendly layout


## PHASE 4: Solve the "first load" problem (Week 3-4)
[ ] Model is cached after first download, so later visits load in seconds
[ ] Make it a PWA (service worker) so it works offline
[ ] Show a clear warning: "Downloads ~X MB once. Wi-Fi recommended."
[ ] Support resuming interrupted downloads
[ ] Consider a very small default model for a fast first impression


## PHASE 5: Make it smarter without a server (Week 4-6)
[x] In-browser RAG: embed documents with Transformers.js, store vectors in
    IndexedDB, retrieve relevant chunks before answering (src/rag)
[ ] System prompts / modes (tutor, coder, summarizer)
[ ] JS tools: calculator, unit converter, local notes search
[ ] Optional: LoRA fine-tune offline (laptop or free Colab), convert and ship
    as a custom MLC model build


## PHASE 6: Security (Week 6)
[ ] NEVER render model output as raw HTML. Use textContent, or sanitize with
    DOMPurify. Otherwise you create an XSS hole.
[ ] Strict Content-Security-Policy header / meta tag
[ ] Pin library versions and use Subresource Integrity (SRI)
[ ] Host model files yourself or from a trusted source; verify file hashes
    (supply-chain protection against tampered weights)
[ ] Prompt injection still applies with RAG/tools: never let the model
    trigger dangerous actions automatically
[ ] Keep all data local; add a "Clear all data" button
[ ] Mini threat model: XSS, malicious uploaded documents, poisoned model
    files, malicious third-party scripts


## PHASE 7: Test and launch (Week 7-8)
[ ] Test on: low-end phone, mid laptop, high-end laptop
[ ] Test in: Chrome, Edge, Safari, Firefox (WebGPU support varies)
[ ] Measure: load time, tokens per second, memory use
[ ] Re-run the 20-30 test prompts on the final build
[ ] Deploy to GitHub Pages or Cloudflare Pages
[ ] Soft launch to a few users, collect feedback, iterate


## KNOWN LIMITS
- WebGPU works best in recent Chrome and Edge; Safari/Firefox vary by version
- Low-RAM phones may crash on models above ~1B
- Quality is below large cloud models; match the task to the model size


## STARTER STACK (all free)
Runtime  : WebLLM + wllama fallback
Model    : ~1B-3B, 4-bit quantized
Frontend : Plain HTML/JS, or React + Vite
Storage  : IndexedDB
Hosting  : GitHub Pages / Cloudflare Pages
Offline  : Service worker (PWA)


## MINIMAL SKELETON (index.html)

<!doctype html>
<html>
<body>
  <div id="log"></div>
  <input id="q" placeholder="Ask something"><button id="go">Send</button>
  <script type="module">
    import * as webllm from "https://esm.run/@mlc-ai/web-llm";

    const log = document.getElementById("log");
    const modelId = "Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct-q4f16_1-MLC";

    const engine = await webllm.CreateMLCEngine(modelId, {
      initProgressCallback: (p) => { log.textContent = p.text; }
    });

    document.getElementById("go").onclick = async () => {
      const q = document.getElementById("q").value;
      const stream = await engine.chat.completions.create({
        messages: [{ role: "user", content: q }],
        stream: true
      });
      log.textContent = "";
      for await (const chunk of stream) {
        log.textContent += chunk.choices[0]?.delta?.content || "";
      }
    };
  </script>
</body>
</html>

Note: verify the model ID against the library's current model list.


## LATER UPGRADES
- Multi-model routing (tiny model for easy questions, bigger for hard ones)
- Voice input/output with in-browser speech models
- Image understanding with small vision models
- Shared prompt/persona library
- Distillation: train a small model to imitate a larger one, then ship it
